  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nHigh pressure continues to push through the forecast area this \nafternoon. This has allowed for high temperatures to warm to the \nupper 70s and 80s while winds remain light. These pleasant \nconditions will persist for the rest of today. Winds will remain \nelevated through the night as low level pressure gradients tighten \nas the surface high pressure system passes off to the southeast. \nSome fog is possible for locations east of I-29 tonight where the \nlightest winds will reside.\n\nSunday will be a slightly warmer day with high temperatures warming \nto the 80s to low 90s. The low level pressure gradients will tighten \na bit more for the day, resulting in southerly gusts up to 20-35 \nmph. The strongest gusts will occur west of I-29, an especially west \nof the James River. After a breezy Summer day, an incoming upper \nlevel wave will result in a strengthening low level jet for the \nevening and overnight hours. This will keep winds elevated through \nthe night and low temperatures on the low side with lows only \nfalling to the upper 50s and 60s. A few locations could see lows \nbottom out around 70F.\n\nThe next chance for showers and thunderstorms look to return to the \narea on Monday. Some uncertainty remains as a cold front will be \nsituated west/northwest of the forecast area. Storms could develop \non the front during the afternoon and evening timeframe but this \npotential is uncertain as a stout elevated mixed layer (EML) will be \nin place, creating a large cap. The latest REFS supports this as it \nshows a straight 100% chance for convective inhibition (CIN) values \nof -100 J/kg or less. However, some of the latest models show the \nfront pushing through the forecast area during the overnight hours, \nwhich could lead to some convective development along the elevated \nportion of the front. Tough to say if this potential convection will \nbe strong to severe as even the elevated portion of the front may \nreside just ahead of the greater vertical shear. Something to watch \nover the next day or two. Otherwise, Monday will be near to above \nseasonable with highs in the 80s to low 90s.\n\nThe elevated front will slowly push through the rest of the forecast \narea on Tuesday. With continued shortwaves passing over the elevated \nfront, rain looks to persist along the front for the entirety of \nTuesday. The ensembles support this as they show a 30-70% chance for \nrainfall totals to exceed a tenth of an inch near and south of I-90. \nAny appreciable instability looks to reside southeast of the \nforecast area, which should keep severe storm chances at bay. Can't \nrule out a stray rumble of thunder though. Widespread cloud cover \nand rain will keep temperatures cooler, down to the 70s across the \narea. \n\nSome light rain could continue through Wednesday before completely \nexiting the area. Wednesday will see highs below average again in \nthe 70s before highs warm back up to near seasonable in the 80s to \nlow 90s on Thursday, Friday, and Saturday. Chances for rain are \nuncertain for the end of next week due to the increasing variance in \nthe upper level pattern. But if latest trends of broad troughing \nover the Northern Plains holds, then rain chances could perhaps \nincrease as we get closer to this period of time.",
